  "settingsUsageDoc": "Usage guide",
  "settingsUsageDocHint": "How each part works and what its limits are: AI, review, knowledge base, cognitive model, ranking",
  "docAiTitle": "What the assistant can do, and what waits for your consent",
  "docAiBody": "The AI can only change data through tools, and every tool carries a risk level: read-only calls (query_content, fetch_page, evaluate_problem_fit) run immediately; writes (creating tags, flashcards, tasks, documents...) wait for your consent in both the per-action and the plan-first modes; deletions ask every single time and can be undone. You see what a call will change before it runs and get one undo after it; the model never sees your API key, and skill scripts do not receive it either.",
  "docReviewTitle": "Review and flashcards",
  "docReviewBody": "Flashcards hang off tags, and the tag tree is the deck: a daily new/review cap set on one tag covers its whole subtree (no row at all means no cap). Due dates come from FSRS alone - the cognitive model only changes what today's queue puts first. Grading can be strict, lenient or self-assessed; lenient ignores typos and word order, and a card you got wrong ignores the daily cap and comes back the same day.",
  "docKnowledgeTitle": "Knowledge base and tags",
  "docKnowledgeBody": "Tags form a tree, with / separating the levels. Deleting a tag also deletes its whole subtree together with the flashcards and review history inside it (the AI's delete tool asks for each one and leaves an undoable snapshot). Both card shapes - cards generated from a template and cards created directly per unit - count on the cognitive readings page, and the orphan banner there cleans up only when you press its button.",
  "docCognitiveTitle": "How to read the cognitive model",
  "docCognitiveBody": "Cognitive model in settings is a read-only readings page: R is current memory strength, R0 is the strength right after learning, Sigma is accumulated effort, and the developing-zone / dead-end column is the tierB diffusion verdict on the tag graph (starts are the most recently reviewed points, targets are the ones due today); with no tags or no usable start and target it reads unavailable, which is not the same as healthy. \"Danger\" needs confidence data this app does not store, so it never appears. The statements at the top record that it does not move due dates, that its parameters are uncalibrated and that it does not decide for you. The AI's evaluate_problem_fit tool reads the same numbers to judge whether a question is worth doing now.",
  "docFormulaTitle": "How the score is computed",
  "docFormulaBody": "Total = urgency x weight + goal match x weight + state fit x weight + expected pressure x weight - fatigue penalty x weight. Every component is between 0 and 1, the weights are normalized internally, so the total is between 0 and 1 as well. The order is only recomputed when you press the sort button - nothing changes in the background.",
  "docCurrentWeights": "Current weights (shipped values in brackets)",
  "docWeightsNormalized": "Edited weights are normalized automatically: even if they do not add up to 1, the total never exceeds 1 because the ranker rescales by their sum.",
  "docStatusTitle": "Event bar edge colours",
  "docStatusBody": "Green = completed; blue = waiting (expected moment not reached); yellow = expected moment has passed; red = deadline has passed. The colour only expresses state and never changes the ordering.",

